"""Respond to grammatical links, especially East Asian topic/condition markers.

Japanese は ('as for'), も ('also'), and compounds with these particles
have the largest responses. Conditional endings are also strong; ordinary
case particles are weaker. Korean topic and conditional endings behave
similarly. Japanese demonstratives have a negative response. Outside these
scripts, the principal response is to the English past copula was/were.
This is a current-token approximation, not a syntactic parser: small tables
cover grammatical words, and suffix rules handle fused tokens. It ignores
context-dependent strength and most weak lexical responses. Otherwise,
pure hiragana gets a small positive baseline and other tokens get zero.
Coefficients are rounded training averages, not grammatical probabilities.
"""
